WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.570
With Taskmaster set to step into the upcoming
Black Widow movie, it's time to dive into

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:09.820
a long history that's strange even by the
standards of the Marvel Universe.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:15.660
From a school for supervillains to the South
American village populated by Hitler clones,

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.860
here's the truth behind the Taskmaster.

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:25.080
The Taskmaster made his first appearance in
1980's Avengers #195, arriving on the last

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:28.000
page to set up his full appearance in the
next issue.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:34.040
Created by David Michelinie and George Pérez,
his entire identity was built around one simple

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:37.620
idea: Anything you can do, he can do just
like you can.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:43.389
The Taskmaster's power is usually referred
to as "photographic reflexes," which is a

00:00:43.389 --> 00:00:48.059
fancy way of saying that if he sees someone
doing something, he can replicate that action

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:51.320
exactly, and he keeps that skill for the rest
of his life.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:56.339
That would be a pretty impressive ability
in the real world, but Taskmaster lives in

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:57.460
the Marvel Universe.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:02.399
He can settle in with some footage of Spider-Man
or Black Panther, and hop off the couch a

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.410
few hours later with the ability to perfectly
replicate their movements.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:11.900
Of course, there's one interesting limit to
all this: photographic reflexes are Taskmaster's

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:12.860
only power.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:17.980
That means that while he can watch Captain
America throwing his shield or Iron Fist doing

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:20.820
kung fu, he can only replicate the movements.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:25.940
He doesn't have the Super Soldier Serum-enhanced
strength behind the throw or the ability to

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:28.860
channel the power of an immortal dragon into
his hand.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.620
The same goes for more mundane abilities,
too.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:36.760
While he can spend all day watching Wolverine,
he's never going to have an adamantium -coated

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:37.260
skeleton.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.680
Still, he's come away from his battles against
the Avengers with some pretty impressive abilities.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:48.770
While he's done his share of hands-on villainy,
the Taskmaster's true calling has always been

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:49.440
education.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:54.820
Of course, Taskmaster's version of education
isn't all that selfless, especially when you

00:01:54.820 --> 00:02:00.070
consider that his "school," the Taskmaster
Academy, is less of a learning institution

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:03.440
and more of a for-profit training center for
henchmen.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:08.960
For a low price, Taskmaster takes on classes
of aspiring supervillains, and teaches them

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:14.100
how to not only fight like superheroes, but
more importantly, how to fight against them.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:18.620
There's no better way to prepare for fighting
Captain America than by training with a guy

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.400
who can fight exactly like Captain America.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:26.400
While this mostly functions as a pretty amazing
explanation for where all the masked members

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.560
of Hydra and AIM get the confidence to try
to punch out Iron Man or whomever, it's worth

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:36.160
noting that it's not just the bad guys who've
taken advantage of the Taskmaster's skills.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:41.700
Taskmaster was also responsible for training
John Walker, better known as U.S. Agent, during

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:46.650
the latter's brief stint as Captain America,
and for training government superhero recruits

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:48.080
in Avengers: The Initiative.

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:50.540
"We're working...

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:55.560
Class dismissed."

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.900
Like Deadpool, the Taskmaster made his living
as a super-powered mercenary for hire when

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:06.419
he wasn't running henchman classes, meaning
that the two characters ran in pretty similar

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:07.040
circles.

00:03:07.669 --> 00:03:12.200
While their first encounter didn't exactly
end on the best of terms, they'd end up encountering

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.820
each other pretty often, and that eventually
led to a friendship.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:20.180
One of the Taskmaster's most prominent roles
in the comics came when he was a member of

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:21.800
Deadpool's supporting cast.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:26.320
His frequent appearances were mostly due to
the fact that Deadpool hired Taskmaster's

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:29.659
ex-girlfriend, Sandi Brandenberg, to be his
secretary.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:34.959
But when Deadpool blew up, lost his memory,
changed his codename to Agent X, and was then

00:03:34.959 --> 00:03:40.019
revealed to have not been Deadpool all along,
Taskmaster helped to re-train him as an assassin

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:41.880
and later fought alongside him.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:46.780
In more recent years, the two mercenaries
have had a more back-and-forth frenemy relationship.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:51.280
They've occasionally come to blows while on
opposite sides of a conflict, but Deadpool

00:03:51.281 --> 00:03:56.139
was also willing to help Taskmaster out by
going easy on him in a fight so that Taskmaster

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:57.100
could still get paid.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.460
He even invited Taskmaster to his bachelor
party!

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:06.080
Taskmaster's status as a sort of utility player
of the supervillain world has led to more

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.780
than a few minor roles in projects outside
of comics.

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:14.120
The most prominent one, at least until Black
Widow hits theaters in 2020, has been his

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:17.480
presence in 2018's Spider-Man for PlayStation
4.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:22.520
In the game, the Taskmaster occasionally shows
up to challenge Spider-Man, but he does so

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:25.420
in a way that suits how his character works
in the comics.

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:30.380
Rather than confronting him directly at first,
Taskmaster puts Spidey through a series of

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:31.080
challenges.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:35.590
Presumably, he does this so that he can get
a good look at Spidey in battle, which leads

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:40.860
to the fight where he actually does show up,
doing a pretty passable impression of Spider-Man's

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:41.540
own moves.

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:42.860
"If you know…

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:47.280
If you can."

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:52.100
While he's mostly shown up as a villain or
a supporting character, the Taskmaster has

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:56.960
dabbled in being a protagonist in his own
right thanks to two self-titled miniseries.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:01.940
The first is a relatively straightforward
action adventure, but his second miniseries

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:03.460
is full-on bananas.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:08.720
The main premise is that the Taskmaster, ironically
enough, has a memory problem.

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:14.530
His brain is so loaded up with superhero moves
that his muscle memory has overwritten other,

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:16.460
less important things, like his past.

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:21.620
In order to keep himself centered, he has
to keep tracking down his own memories, following

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:25.120
his history so that he can piece it together
and figure out who he is.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:29.040
It's sort of like Memento, only a million
times weirder.

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:33.480
The path to Taskmaster's memories leads him
through things like an encounter with Don

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:38.360
of the Dead, a Latin-American drug dealer
who turns out to be a white American S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:42.100
agent who'd taken the "Don of the Dead" identity
after Taskmaster trained him.

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:47.900
That, however, pales in comparison to where
that series ends up: Wewelsburg II, a small

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:53.280
town in the Bolivian Andes where Nazi scientists
escaped to after World War II in order to

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:55.720
continue their experiments cloning Hitler.

00:05:56.140 --> 00:06:00.830
The most successful result was the long-time
Marvel villain the Hate-Monger, but the scientists

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:05.570
wound up with a gigantic stockpile of cloned
Hitler-brains, which leaked the Fürher's

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:07.900
"bio-chemical essence" into the local water
supply.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:09.080
The result?

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:14.520
Every man, woman, and child in the town essentially
has Hitler's brain, leading to a constant

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:17.100
warfare among themselves for control of the
town.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:23.300
Beyond Wewelsburg II and the Don of the Dead,
the big reveal of the Taskmaster series is

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:27.250
that it finally gives us the Taskmaster's
origin and his real name.

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:31.820
That was what he'd forgotten, the piece of
knowledge that sent him on that bizarre quest,

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:34.100
and the answers change almost everything.

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:39.200
For years, the Taskmaster had been getting
his orders from a secret organization called

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:39.760
"the Org."

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:42.660
But as it turns out, the Org doesn't exist.

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:48.180
Instead, it's the wife that Taskmaster doesn't
remember having, Mercedes Merced, who was

00:06:48.181 --> 00:06:50.770
also his partner back when he was a S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:51.880
agent named Tony Masters.

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:56.920
As Nick Fury tells Steve Rogers, the Taskmaster
has been a good guy all along.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:58.340
He just doesn't remember it

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:03.000
As Masters, he was sent to infiltrate the
supervillain community for S.H.I.E.L.D., using

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:06.880
his powers and creating the Taskmaster identity
as his cover.

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:11.660
Unfortunately, thanks to a flawed prototype
of the Super Soldier Serum that expanded his

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:16.140
powers, all of those skills he'd absorbed
overwrote the personal parts of his memory.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:22.060
Mercedes has been minimizing the damage for
years by creating the Org, acting as Taskmaster's

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:24.700
handler, and pointing him in the right directions
for S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:29.520
Of course, once he discovers all this, he
immediately has to absorb yet another sequence

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:34.650
of physical actions, forgetting the revelations
about Tony Masters and putting a heartbroken

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:39.420
Mercedes right back into the role of working
with a husband who doesn't remember she exists.

00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:45.080
While it's doubtful that the Don of the Dead
or the Taskmaster Academy's acclaimed henchman

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:50.030
resource program will make it into the MCU,
it's not difficult to imagine how Taskmaster's

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:53.160
presence could be felt in the upcoming Black
Widow movie.

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:57.750
Most of Natasha Romanoff's history has to
do with her training in the Red Room, a secret

00:07:57.750 --> 00:08:02.150
Soviet program designed to create a series
of highly trained agents who are given the

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:03.740
codename of Black Widow.

00:08:04.030 --> 00:08:08.580
In addition to being briefly shown in Avengers:
Age of Ultron, the Red Room also produced

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:12.920
Peggy Carter's nemesis, Dottie Underwood,
on the Agent Carter TV show.

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:17.720
With the Taskmaster's history of training
supervillains, and his ability to serve as

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:22.490
the perfect teacher in virtually any style
of combat, it's easy to picture him as one

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:23.660
of Natasha's trainers.

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:29.540
Even more than the visual, the Black Widow
version of the Taskmaster captures the character's

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:34.280
signature abilities with an accuracy that's
really exciting, both for longtime comics

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:38.020
readers and the MCU fans who have never picked
up a comic in their lives.

00:08:38.409 --> 00:08:43.040
Moviegoers have seen these characters in action
for over a decade, and whether they realize

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:48.210
it or not, they've seen how those movies distinguish
their characters in ways that go beyond their

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:48.700
costumes.

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:54.120
Captain America, Black Panther, and the Winter
Soldier, for instance, all essentially have

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:58.490
the same set of superpowers but they each
have a fighting style that doesn't look like

00:08:58.490 --> 00:08:59.140
the others.

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:03.730
You're not going to see Black Panther deliver
that one sternum-shattering Muay Thai kick

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:08.310
like Cap does, and you're not going to see
Bucky Barnes pull off the same acrobatic attacks

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:10.040
that T'Challa busts out, either.

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:13.320
You will, however, see the Taskmaster do it
all.

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:18.020
In the Black Widow trailer alone, we see him
perfectly mimicking Captain America throwing

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:23.000
his shield, Hawkeye's draw with his bow, and
Black Panther's claws, among other moves.

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:28.120
What's more, Black Widow seems to be making
an effort to not just copy the style, but

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:30.740
to mimic specific moments from earlier films.

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:35.160
The shot with his claws in particular bears
a striking resemblance to a shot from the

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:37.720
war sequence in Avengers: Infinity War.

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:43.220
Much like the way that the comic book Taskmaster
ran his school for aspiring supervillains,

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:46.560
the cinematic version has also embraced his
calling as a teacher.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:51.360
This time, he's the guy running the Red Room,
the top-secret Russian training facility that

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:54.140
produces the super-spies of the Black Widow
program.

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:59.140
As a result, he's someone that our most familiar
Black Widow, Natasha Romanoff, knows from

00:09:59.140 --> 00:09:59.640
her past.

00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:04.760
He's also apparently responsible for training
Yelena Belova, the second Black Widow in the

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:09.840
comics who initially set out to eliminate
and replace Natasha long after the original's

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:11.080
defection to S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:16.040
The fact that it's the Taskmaster, however,
adds another layer to the story of Black Widow

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:19.020
returning to the Red Room with the intention
of taking it down.

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:23.860
Not only does the Taskmaster's ability to
replicate any movement that he sees make him

00:10:23.860 --> 00:10:28.550
the perfect combat instructor, it also hints
at something that might just be the true purpose

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:30.520
of the modern MCU Red Room.

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:35.620
Outside of a brief, hallucinatory flashback
in Avengers: Age of Ultron, we never really

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:40.390
see much of what Natasha's training was like
in the MCU, and what we do see seems like

00:10:40.390 --> 00:10:41.820
some pretty standard stuff.

00:10:42.450 --> 00:10:46.240
The fact that Taskmaster can fight like the
Avengers, though, might just mean that this

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:50.300
resurrected Red Room isn't just training its
soldiers to take on enemy spies.

00:10:50.860 --> 00:10:55.300
In the comics, the thing that makes training
with the Taskmaster such an alluring prospect

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:59.360
is that if you're sparring with a guy who
fights exactly like Captain America, then

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:02.060
you might just figure out how to beat Captain
America.

00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:06.420
If that sounds like a long shot, consider
that Cap and the Winter Soldier, the product

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:11.710
of a similar, Red Room-adjacent program, laid
a beatdown on Iron Man that was about as devastating

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:13.300
as the one Thanos did.

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:18.150
If the Red Room is turning out soldiers designed
specifically to take down superheroes, then

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:22.529
there's a very good reason why Natasha would
want to take them down that goes well beyond

00:11:22.529 --> 00:11:25.060
revenge and atoning for her own dark past.

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:29.560
We'll just have to wait and see if the Taskstudent
can defeat the Taskmaster.

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:31.380
"Look around…

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:37.200
The day their heroes failed them."

